WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.200
Good morning Hank, it's Tuesday.

00:00:01.300 --> 00:00:04.400
I recently became intensely interested in a question.

00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:10.460
What was the most recent year in which the overall global population of humans declined?

00:00:10.680 --> 00:00:14.120
I don't even remember why I was initially curious about this, but I was

00:00:14.120 --> 00:00:18.300
and I couldn't figure it out, despite some pretty deep googling and a visit to the library

00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:24.060
So eventually I asked Max Roser who works at my favorite website Our World in Data. But he didn't know either.

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:27.320
One of his colleagues, however, Hannah Richie did find an answer.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:32.360
The last time the global human population decreased year over year was 1648,

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:37.740
and the story of 1648 turns out to be really interesting and also like

00:00:38.220 --> 00:00:38.900
relevant.

00:00:39.220 --> 00:00:44.860
Ok, so the global human population actually decreased every year between 1608 and 1648,

00:00:44.860 --> 00:00:48.000
part of a period sometimes known as "The General Crisis"

00:00:48.000 --> 00:00:51.680
which I would say is an example of historians being terrible at naming things

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:54.680
Except that it was a rather general crisis.

00:00:54.680 --> 00:00:56.760
From Africa to Asia to Europe to the Americas

00:00:56.760 --> 00:01:00.420
there was quite a lot of crisis going on. Which is a little weird, because

00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:05.180
overall, the human species was getting much wealthier and better connected.

00:01:05.180 --> 00:01:08.620
Like for instance, there was much more silver because European empires,

00:01:08.620 --> 00:01:12.320
especially the Spanish Empire, were extracting it from colonies in the Americas.

00:01:12.440 --> 00:01:17.040
There was also a much broader range of food available, rice and wheat had just come to the Americas,

00:01:17.040 --> 00:01:21.860
and everything from pineapples to cassava to potatoes had come to afro-eurasia.

00:01:21.860 --> 00:01:27.280
And you would think with more money and more food there would be more people, but no.

00:01:27.280 --> 00:01:30.160
For one thing the Columbian Exchange also brought devastation

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:35.160
perhaps more than 90% of Native Americans were killed in the centuries after Europeans arrived.

00:01:35.160 --> 00:01:39.500
Mostly from diseases that were new to the Americas like smallpox and diphtheria.

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:43.740
That population decline began around 1500 but lasted throughout The General Crisis

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:49.340
and then there was the Atlantic slave trade which was growing throughout The General Crisis with horrific consequences of its own.

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:53.220
Around 15 percent of Africans died in slave ships on the journey to the Americas

00:01:53.220 --> 00:01:56.720
and those who didn't saw their life expectancies severely diminished

00:01:56.720 --> 00:02:01.400
and I think it's worth noting that many of the crops that relied on slave labor like coffee and tobacco

00:02:01.420 --> 00:02:04.331
were not necessary to sustain human life.

00:02:04.340 --> 00:02:06.860
They were basically luxury goods,

00:02:06.860 --> 00:02:10.360
so maybe more access to luxury goods indicated that humanity was getting wealthier,

00:02:10.380 --> 00:02:14.300
but that doesn't necessarily mean that on average human life was getting better.

00:02:14.300 --> 00:02:18.440
In fact, despite the influx of wealth, European populations were also declining,

00:02:18.440 --> 00:02:23.440
largely due to the 30 Years War. One of the longest and deadliest wars in European history.

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:25.060
Which is really saying something.

00:02:25.060 --> 00:02:28.920
The war killed through combat and famine around 20% of people in what is now Germany

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:32.480
and as many as a third of people in what are now Poland and Lithuania.

00:02:32.480 --> 00:02:37.240
Meanwhile Spain, which had become phenomenally wealthy from all that silver, defaulted on its debts

00:02:37.240 --> 00:02:43.740
repeatedly during the general crisis because they didn't understand inflation, and all that silver caused a lot of it.

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:47.520
but the single biggest contributor to 17th century population decline

00:02:47.580 --> 00:02:50.620
was the collapse of the Ming Dynasty in China.

00:02:50.620 --> 00:02:54.100
Between 1600 and 1644 when the Qing dynasty began,

00:02:54.220 --> 00:02:57.840
China's population decreased by 50 million people

00:02:57.840 --> 00:03:01.360
and that was also partly due to humanity's newfound abundance of silver.

00:03:01.360 --> 00:03:04.060
The Ming government required people to pay taxes in silver,

00:03:04.060 --> 00:03:09.100
which meant more people had to spend more hours working at making goods that could be sold for silver,

00:03:09.100 --> 00:03:11.400
which meant lower agricultural yields.

00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:16.160
Also the Ming Dynasty, like Spain, failed to understand inflation all of which led to famine.

00:03:16.160 --> 00:03:19.480
One more thing that made The General Crisis so general and crisis-e,

00:03:19.540 --> 00:03:22.780
at the time earth experienced significant climate change.

00:03:22.780 --> 00:03:24.540
It's sometimes called the Little Ice Age

00:03:24.600 --> 00:03:29.080
global temperatures dropped on average around one degree Celsius, which also contributed to famines,

00:03:29.400 --> 00:03:30.520
but don't worry It's not like

00:03:30.720 --> 00:03:31.220
oh

00:03:31.420 --> 00:03:32.080
It is like

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:36.206
Hank since the general crisis humanity has experienced its longest ever run of

00:03:36.206 --> 00:03:40.160
population growth, increased life expectancy ,and decreased poverty

00:03:40.160 --> 00:03:46.320
but the early 17th century is a reminder that progress is never simple or universal or guaranteed.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:50.260
It's a reminder that a changing climate can fuel global instability and that

00:03:50.260 --> 00:03:54.360
growing connectivity and wealth is only good news for humans.

00:03:54.400 --> 00:03:57.880
Once we learn to live with and share that growth.

00:03:57.880 --> 00:04:00.420
Hank, I'll see you on Friday.

